    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.delete_nodes_ws - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.delete_nodes_ws.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.delete_nodes_ws()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Delete all selected nodes and their incident edges.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.delete_nodes_ws()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Delete all selected nodes and their incident edges.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.delete_nodes_ws()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Delete all selected nodes and their incident edges.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nDelete all selected nodes and their incident edges.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L3310-L3423){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.delete_nodes_ws()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Delete selected nodes](../assets/graphs/delete-nodes-ws.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a six-node graph with nodes B and E selected in the accent color. Right: B and E are gone along with all five of their incident edges, shown as dashed outlines; only A, C, D, F and the edges A–D and C–F remain.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nRemoves nodes in the current selection from the graph, along with all edges connected to\nthose nodes. Updates selection state and removes the selected nodes from the graph\nstructure.\n\nThe `_ws` suffix stands for \"with selection\": rather than taking nodes as arguments, the\nmethod acts on whatever nodes are currently selected. This separates *which* nodes to\nremove (decided by any of the `select_nodes*()` methods, traversals like `trav_out()`, or\nselection set operations) from the removal itself, which makes condition-based pruning\neasy, for example:\n\n- dropping low-degree or isolated nodes before an analysis\n- removing nodes whose attributes mark them as noise or out of scope\n- cutting away a neighborhood reached by a traversal\n\nAfter deletion the removed nodes are no longer part of the node selection (it ends up\nempty), and their removed edges are no longer part of the edge selection. If no nodes are\nselected, the call does nothing. The graph is modified in place and, when at least one node\nis removed, the deletion is recorded as a single step in the step log (see `steps`). To\ndelete just the selected edges while keeping all nodes, use `delete_edges_ws()`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Self for method chaining.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~select_nodes`) \n\n:   Select nodes with a predicate.\n\n[](`~delete_node`) \n\n:   Remove one node.\n\n[](`~delete_edges_ws`) \n\n:   Delete the selected edges.\n\n[](`~transform_to_subgraph_ws`) \n\n:   Keep only the selected nodes.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nDeletion acts on whatever nodes are currently selected, so select the nodes to remove\nfirst. Deleting the middle node of a five-node path removes its two edges too, and\n`delete_nodes_ws()` empties the selection, so calling it again does nothing:\n\n::: {#b8c080dd .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(5)  # path: 1-2-3-4-5\n\ngraph.select_nodes_by_id([graph.get_id_from_label(\"3\")])\ngraph.delete_nodes_ws()\ngraph.delete_nodes_ws()\n\ngraph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges(), graph.get_selected_node_ids()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(4, 2, [])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Payments between people\n\n`payments()` has two kinds of account, people and businesses. Selecting the businesses\nwith a predicate on their `kind` and deleting them leaves the payments between people.\nLaying out the whole network first, with `layout_kamada_kawai()`, keeps every person\nwhere they were in it:\n\n::: {#3ab112c2 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments = ec.payments()\npayments.layout_kamada_kawai(weighted=False)\n\n\ndef is_business(node):\n    return node.attributes[\"kind\"] == \"business\"\n\n\npayments.select_nodes(is_business)\npayments.delete_nodes_ws()\n\npayments.count_nodes(), payments.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(14, 19)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWithout the wages, the shopping and the rent, what remains is the two circles of large\npayments, the chain from Cruz to Lena and the pairs who pay each other:\n\n::: {#405290db .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments.plot(layout=\"pos\", edge_width_by=\"weight\", label_by=\"label\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#37